WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:03.769 --> 00:00:06.540
Welcome to the United States, welcome to Los
Angeles,

00:00:06.540 --> 00:00:11.450
and welcome to the world-famous [BLEEP].

00:00:11.450 --> 00:00:13.690
Trademark rights are an interesting thing.

00:00:13.690 --> 00:00:17.529
You may already know about copyright, about
it's been extended in the US and Europe many,

00:00:17.529 --> 00:00:22.910
many times, and always -- strangely enough
-- just before the early Mickey Mouse cartoons

00:00:22.910 --> 00:00:27.910
were about to become public domain. But that's
copyright, not trademarking. Separately to

00:00:27.910 --> 00:00:32.960
those cartoons, the name and basic design
of Mickey Mouse is also trademarked. It's

00:00:32.960 --> 00:00:38.060
something that Disney use to sell... well,
many, many, many things. So even if those

00:00:38.060 --> 00:00:42.030
early cartoons were to go out of copyright,
I couldn't start selling my own Mickey Mouse

00:00:42.030 --> 00:00:46.610
merchandise, the same way I can't just set
up a store called Wal-Mart or start selling

00:00:46.610 --> 00:00:52.260
my own drink called Coca-Cola. The rights
to the names and designs don't have an expiry

00:00:52.260 --> 00:00:54.620
date, as long as they're being used and defended.

00:00:54.620 --> 00:00:59.610
The distinction is about commercial purpose.
Which is why I've blurred the [BLEEP] behind

00:00:59.610 --> 00:01:03.250
me. You already know what it is and what it
looks like, it's one of the most recognisable

00:01:03.250 --> 00:01:08.969
landmarks in America. You can easily find
photos and videos of it all over the web,

00:01:08.969 --> 00:01:14.799
even videos of people climbing it and being
chased off by the police. But am I using it

00:01:14.799 --> 00:01:20.810
for a commercial purpose by making this video
about it? I don't think so, I'm using it nominatively,

00:01:20.810 --> 00:01:26.729
to describe the thing itself, which is legal.
But am I willing to risk it when there are

00:01:26.729 --> 00:01:30.850
lawsuits from the trademark owner and out-of-court
settlements going back decades?

00:01:30.850 --> 00:01:35.340
Now, could I have gotten permission if I'd
made a couple of phone calls? Yeah, maybe,

00:01:35.340 --> 00:01:39.329
but they'd have probably have charged me a
fee and wanted me to sign a lot of paperwork,

00:01:39.329 --> 00:01:45.329
and frankly, I'm on vacation. Am I likely
to get sued if I actually show you it? Probably not.

00:01:45.329 --> 00:01:51.389
But am I absolutely, 100% sure that the
trademark owners wouldn't go after me if I

00:01:51.389 --> 00:01:55.639
showed you one of the most recognisable landmarks
in America? And that I wouldn't have to settle

00:01:55.639 --> 00:01:59.509
out of court or risk the cost of defending
a very expensive lawsuit?

00:01:59.509 --> 00:02:02.939
No. I'm not 100% sure.

00:02:02.939 --> 00:02:06.859
That's the reason that there's just a blur
of pixels behind me.

00:02:06.859 --> 00:02:10.640
I'm out here in LA for another few days. If
any YouTube types want to collaborate on something

00:02:10.640 --> 00:02:15.010
on November 6th or 7th, send me an email,
details are on screen now. I don't have much

00:02:15.010 --> 00:02:19.280
time but it'd be great to do something, so
do get in touch.

00:02:19.280 --> 00:02:21.250
Oh my god, it's bright out here.

00:02:22.000 --> 00:02:24.000
[Translating these subtitles? Add your name here!]

